WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.434
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy

00:00:02.434 --> 00:00:04.701
and Tony Award-winning actor
and one of the stars

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:06.734
of "Dicks: The Musical."

00:00:06.734 --> 00:00:08.167
The soundtrack is available now

00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:10.667
and the film opens nationwide
this Friday.

00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:12.033
Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:15.601
our very good friend
Nathan Lane, everybody.

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:17.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:24.634
♪♪

00:00:24.634 --> 00:00:29.400
♪♪

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.601
♪♪

00:00:33.601 --> 00:00:35.667
Welcome back, Nathan.

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:38.267
-Oh, it's nice to be back.
Nice to see you.

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:39.834
-You're back
to your snazzy self.

00:00:39.834 --> 00:00:42.000
You're wearing a pocket square.
You look like $1 million.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.501
-Oh, thank you.
Thank you very much.

00:00:45.501 --> 00:00:47.367
You, too, as always.

00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:51.033
[ Laughter ]

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:53.734
-You were here right
before the writers strike.

00:00:53.734 --> 00:00:56.567
And so we've been apart
for a little over five months.

00:00:56.567 --> 00:00:58.033
-I know, I know.
-Did you use the time well?

00:00:58.033 --> 00:01:00.234
-Well, you know,
I'm very happy

00:01:00.234 --> 00:01:01.667
the writers strike
resolved favorably

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:02.834
for the writers, of course.

00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:05.834
And more importantly,
that it brought you back

00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:08.067
to late night where you belong.
-Oh, thank you.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.367
-I --
Oh, there was one clap.

00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:13.701
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.701 --> 00:01:14.834
Wow.

00:01:14.834 --> 00:01:17.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.501
Because...

00:01:18.501 --> 00:01:21.934
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.934 --> 00:01:23.767
Because quite -- quite honestly,

00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:25.200
I didn't know
what to do with myself

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.534
when you weren't doing --
doing the show.

00:01:27.534 --> 00:01:31.534
No, no, I was getting
far too much sleep.

00:01:31.534 --> 00:01:34.934
I became downright perky,
and no one wants to see that.

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:38.200
I was so perky I had to rewatch
"The Crown" just to stabilize.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.434
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.434 --> 00:01:40.934
-Oh, to bring it back down?
-Yeah.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:44.100
Without you, the only thing
keeping me up at night

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.834
was flashbacks
of the Lauren Boebert video,

00:01:46.834 --> 00:01:49.501
where she was vaping
and groping

00:01:49.501 --> 00:01:51.934
her boyfriend during
"Beetlejuice: The Musical."

00:01:51.934 --> 00:01:53.868
-Yes, of course,
-Perhaps Republicans

00:01:53.868 --> 00:01:56.868
should just avoid
theater altogether.

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:59.868
I'm sure Lincoln would have
agreed with that.

00:01:59.868 --> 00:02:01.701
Nevertheless --
Oh, too soon?

00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:06.868
Nevertheless, I'm sure I'm not
the only "Late Night" viewer

00:02:06.868 --> 00:02:08.868
who built a tiny replica
of your desk

00:02:08.868 --> 00:02:12.501
and put their dog behind it
dressed like you.

00:02:12.501 --> 00:02:14.334
It wasn't quite the same,

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:17.868
but I did enjoy his segment
called "A Closer Woof."

00:02:17.868 --> 00:02:19.534
-Oh,

00:02:19.534 --> 00:02:21.634
-That was a joke
just for Frisbee.

00:02:21.634 --> 00:02:23.100
-Oh, my dog?
-Just for your dog, Frisbee.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.834
-My dog will watch tonight
for the first time

00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:27.167
and she'll get one of the jokes.
-That's right.

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:28.667
-The Lauren Boebert thing
must have been

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:29.701
a little harrowing for you

00:02:29.701 --> 00:02:31.734
being a longtime
Broadway performer.

00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:33.501
Have you ever seen
that sort of --

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:35.434
that sort of beeswax
going on?

00:02:35.434 --> 00:02:37.000
-In the theater?
-Yeah.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.033
-Well -- well, sure.

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:43.534
I mean, you know, there --
No, it's a free for all now.

00:02:43.534 --> 00:02:47.100
No, you know, but speaking
of free for alls,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.834
I just went to see the Madonna
Celebration Tour in London.

00:02:50.834 --> 00:02:52.334
It was astonishing.

00:02:52.334 --> 00:02:54.267
You know, we're approximately
the same age.

00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:56.501
-You and Madonna
-And I find -- me and Madonna.

00:02:56.501 --> 00:02:59.133
-Yeah.
-And what kind of a gay man

00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:02.234
would I be if I didn't go to
the Madonna Celebration tour?

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:04.133
-It's like a celebration
of you too in a way.

00:03:04.133 --> 00:03:05.400
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.667
And I just find her
inspiring on so many levels.

00:03:08.667 --> 00:03:11.033
And while I was watching,
I said to myself,

00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:13.968
"I need to get a pair
of fishnet compression socks."

00:03:13.968 --> 00:03:16.067
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:17.267
But I did think, you know,

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:19.834
it must be tough
to be a rocker at 65

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:22.400
when the only white powder
backstage is Gold Bond.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.734
[ Laughter ]

00:03:27.434 --> 00:03:30.534
Too much voguing can cause
carpal tunnel syndrome.

00:03:30.534 --> 00:03:32.300
-That's true.
-But there she was,

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:34.901
acting like a virgin
touched for the very first time.

00:03:34.901 --> 00:03:36.934
-Oh, good for her.
-It was very moving.

00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:38.734
-Very moving.
Now, you know,

00:03:38.734 --> 00:03:40.901
you talk about the fact
that you're almost

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:42.234
the same age as her,
and that means

00:03:42.234 --> 00:03:43.267
you've had
a very long career.

00:03:43.267 --> 00:03:44.367
We obviously know that.

00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:46.334
Have you been approached
to write a memoir?

00:03:46.334 --> 00:03:48.067
-Yeah, I'm trying, I'm trying.
-Oh, you're trying right now?

00:03:48.067 --> 00:03:50.000
-It's very difficult.
Yeah, yeah.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.234
The hardest parts are
the remembering...

00:03:52.234 --> 00:03:54.100
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.434
...and the writing.
-Yeah.

00:03:56.434 --> 00:03:59.234
-And -- and, oh
and the remembering.

00:03:59.234 --> 00:04:00.968
-[ Laughs ]
-You know, I should have --

00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:03.133
I should have written things
down along the way.

00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:04.801
I should have kept a journal,
but I didn't.

00:04:04.801 --> 00:04:06.367
It's like going into
a grocery store

00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:07.934
and realizing
I should have written a list

00:04:07.934 --> 00:04:10.033
for what I came here for.

00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:11.634
Instead of forgetting bananas,

00:04:11.634 --> 00:04:15.334
I'm forgetting my 20s,
30s and 40s.

00:04:15.334 --> 00:04:18.868
Cosmopolitans may have played
a major factor in that,

00:04:18.868 --> 00:04:20.968
but I don't have
many bombshells

00:04:20.968 --> 00:04:24.567
like -- like Jada Pinkett Smith
revealing she --

00:04:24.567 --> 00:04:26.501
she and Will Smith have been

00:04:26.501 --> 00:04:28.634
secretly separated
for seven years.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:29.834
W.T.F.

00:04:29.834 --> 00:04:31.200
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.934
As the kids would say,
W.T.F.!

00:04:33.934 --> 00:04:37.033
-Oh, wow. You're doing it in a
very believable kids way.

00:04:37.033 --> 00:04:38.467
-Aren't I?

00:04:38.467 --> 00:04:41.467
You know, and still
they -- they work together,

00:04:41.467 --> 00:04:43.834
they raised the children, they
attended events as a couple.

00:04:43.834 --> 00:04:49.167
It seems like maybe
it was a secret even from them.

00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:50.767
-Oh, you don't think
they even knew?

00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:52.734
-I started to wonder
maybe my husband and I

00:04:52.734 --> 00:04:54.868
are secretly separated here.

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:57.734
All this time I just thought
he was in the other room

00:04:57.734 --> 00:05:00.100
watching bad reality shows.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.534
I guess I won't figure it out

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:04.367
until he gets up and slaps
someone at the Tony Awards.

00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:06.501
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.767 --> 00:05:09.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:09.367 --> 00:05:11.167
-Oh, real quick.
-Yeah.

00:05:11.167 --> 00:05:13.100
-Real quick, and then we're
going to go to commercial.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.968
But, you know,
speaking of the Tonys,

00:05:14.968 --> 00:05:17.167
you and I are working
on a -- on a musical.

00:05:17.167 --> 00:05:18.834
-Oh, geez.
-"Cicada, Cicada."

00:05:18.834 --> 00:05:20.300
-Oh, well, that's the elephant
in the room.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.601
-It is the elephant.
-The question on

00:05:21.601 --> 00:05:24.567
everybody's minds is
"Cicada, Cicada: The Musical."

00:05:24.567 --> 00:05:27.334
Now, I'm not --
I'm not privy to everything,

00:05:27.334 --> 00:05:28.868
but I've heard that the script

00:05:28.868 --> 00:05:30.334
and the score
are still in development.

00:05:30.334 --> 00:05:31.434
-Yes.
-And it's not ready

00:05:31.434 --> 00:05:32.834
for a table read.
-No.

00:05:32.834 --> 00:05:35.334
-But I'm pleased to announce
we have picked out a table.

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:37.467
-[ Laughing ] We have.

00:05:37.467 --> 00:05:41.467
-And the good news is the title
is testing through the roof

00:05:41.467 --> 00:05:43.767
with both entomologists
and entomologists.

00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:45.834
-Oh, that's good.

00:05:45.834 --> 00:05:49.801
-[ Laughing ] Because Broadway
has not taken advantage

00:05:49.801 --> 00:05:53.601
of the insect wordplay market

00:05:53.601 --> 00:05:55.601
since "Spider-Man:
Turn Off the Dark."

00:05:55.601 --> 00:05:57.067
-That's true.
-We're on to something.

00:05:57.067 --> 00:05:59.167
-We're on to that.
-But we got to act fast

00:05:59.167 --> 00:06:02.100
because I don't want
some copycat to poach us.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:05.467
And suddenly there's two
cicada-themed musicals

00:06:05.467 --> 00:06:06.734
vying for Broadway.

00:06:06.734 --> 00:06:08.167
-Alright, well,
we're going to get --

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:09.868
we're going to try to get
an actual song

00:06:09.868 --> 00:06:11.701
from "Cicada, Cicada"...
-Yes.

00:06:11.701 --> 00:06:13.767
-...on this show.
The act one finale.

00:06:13.767 --> 00:06:17.634
-The act one finale
within the next six months?

00:06:17.634 --> 00:06:20.367
-Christine Baranski,
Harvey Fierstein and the Mets.

00:06:20.367 --> 00:06:22.734
-Yes, we're going to get
most of the Mets.

00:06:22.734 --> 00:06:24.334
-Most of the Mets.
-Yeah.

00:06:24.334 --> 00:06:26.267
You know, the Mets are
very excited about this.

00:06:26.267 --> 00:06:28.067
-I bet.
-It's when you have --

00:06:28.067 --> 00:06:29.734
when you don't have
postseason baseball

00:06:29.734 --> 00:06:31.801
to look forward to,
you look forward to the stage.

00:06:31.801 --> 00:06:33.367
[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.367 --> 00:06:36.033
I have so much more to ask you
about "Dicks: The Musical,"

00:06:36.033 --> 00:06:37.200
which is a real thing.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.200
We'll be right back
with more Nathan Lane.

